# =============================================================================
# svc CRONTAB — all scheduled app runs on this host
#
# Edit with:  sudo crontab -u svc -e     (never `crontab -u svc <file>` — that
#                                         REPLACES the file and wipes other apps)
# List with:  sudo crontab -u svc -l
#
# ORGANISATION
#   Two sections by CADENCE, not by app:
#     1. DAILY OR MORE FREQUENT  — the steady drumbeat of the host
#     2. LESS THAN DAILY         — weekly / monthly / occasional maintenance
#   Within each section, entries run in CHRONOLOGICAL order — sub-daily jobs by
#   their minute within the hour, infrequent jobs by day and time. Reading top to
#   bottom tells you what happens next. An app with jobs at two cadences appears in
#   BOTH sections. Some jobs are prerequisites of others: keep ordering honest.
#
#   Entries carry ONLY their app header, in the form:
#       # APP-NAME # — WHAT IT DOES, WHEN IT RUNS
#   Anything that needs explaining is either a shared convention (LEGEND, below) or
#   an entry-specific fact (NOTES, bottom). If you add an entry that needs a caveat,
#   add it to NOTES — not above the line.
#
# LEGEND — syntax you will see in the entries below
#   cd /opt/apps/<app>       run from the RELEASE copy, never a dev tree. RELEASE_SHA
#                            'dev-tree' in util.app_run_logs means this was got wrong
#   /usr/bin/docker          ABSOLUTE paths, always. There is no PATH= or SHELL= line
#   /usr/bin/flock           in this crontab, so cron's built-in default applies
#                            (typically /usr/bin:/bin). Bare names would happen to
#                            work — absolute paths mean no entry depends on that, and
#                            no app can break the others by adding a PATH= line later
#   sleep N &&               sub-minute offset; cron granularity is only 1 minute
#   flock -n /tmp/<x>.lock   SKIP this tick if the previous one still holds the lock
#                            (-n = non-blocking; it does not queue). Used ONLY where
#                            overlap can corrupt state — an app that advances a cursor
#                            AFTER doing its work — or where a hang could pile up
#   compose run --rm -T      one-shot container, removed on exit, no TTY
#   >/opt/run-logs/<app>/    SINGLE > , never /dev/null, never >>. An app's real log
#                            sinks only exist once node starts, so a failure before
#                            that (bad image, missing network, dead daemon, wrong
#                            path) leaves no trace anywhere else. Single > bounds the
#                            file to the last invocation
#   2>&1                     stderr follows stdout into the same .out file
#   #<schedule>              a commented-out entry is DORMANT, not deleted — see NOTES
#
#   Not used here: RUN_USER (each entrypoint.sh defaults to svc), HOME= (svc's home
#   is /nonexistent and that is fine).
# =============================================================================


# #############################################################################
# 1. DAILY OR MORE FREQUENT
# #############################################################################

# MMB-RPP # — RUN GROUPS 1-7, EVERY 30 MIN AT :17 AND :47
17,47 * * * * sleep 0  && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.1.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 1 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.1.out 2>&1
17,47 * * * * sleep 10 && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.2.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 2 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.2.out 2>&1
17,47 * * * * sleep 20 && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.3.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 3 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.3.out 2>&1
17,47 * * * * sleep 30 && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.4.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 4 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.4.out 2>&1
17,47 * * * * sleep 40 && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.5.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 5 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.5.out 2>&1
17,47 * * * * sleep 50 && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.6.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 6 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.6.out 2>&1
17,47 * * * * sleep 60 && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.7.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 7 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.7.out 2>&1

# MMB-RPP # — RUN GROUP 0, EVERY 30 MIN AT :20 AND :50
20,50 * * * * sleep 30 && cd /opt/apps/mmb-rpp && /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/mmb-rpp.0.lock /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T mmb-rpp node index.js 0 >/opt/run-logs/mmb-rpp/cron.0.out 2>&1

# ALERT-PROCESSOR # — EVERY 30 MIN AT :24 AND :54
24,54 * * * * cd /opt/apps/alert-processor && /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T alert-processor node index.js >/opt/run-logs/alert-processor/cron.out 2>&1


# #############################################################################
# 2. LESS THAN DAILY
# #############################################################################

# ODD-JOBS # — PARTITION MAINTENANCE, MONTHLY ON THE 1ST AT 14:00
0 14 1 * * cd /opt/apps/odd-jobs && /usr/bin/docker compose run --rm -T pg-part-arch >/opt/run-logs/odd-jobs/cron.pg-part-arch.out 2>&1


# =============================================================================
# NOTES — entry-specific facts. Keyed to the app headers above.
#
# MMB-RPP (groups 1-7, :17/:47)
#   Source captures land at :15 and :45; :17 picks them up ~2 min later while each
#   delta stays roughly 7-15 KB, well under the 400_000-byte guard in runJob READ.
#   The sleep prefixes stagger group starts across :17:00 to :18:00.
#   flock: Redis filesize advances only AFTER a successful insert, so two overlapping
#   runs of the SAME group would both read the same delta and double-insert. Locks
#   are per group — different groups may overlap freely, and since a group takes ~13s
#   expect 2-3 concurrent.
#   The .out files exist to catch pre-node failures only. Switch to /dev/null once
#   proven — but see the ODD-JOBS note before you do that anywhere else.
#
# MMB-RPP (group 0, :20/:50)
#   Own minute slot, ~3 min after the rest, carried over from the legacy prod
#   schedule. Keeps the largest / historically slowest group from contending with
#   groups 1-7.
#
# ALERT-PROCESSOR
#   :24 sits clear of both mmb-rpp windows (groups 1-7 end ~:18:13, group 0 ~:20:43),
#   so no docker contention.
#   No flock: short run relative to a 30-min cadence, and it advances no cursor. If
#   that stops being true, add /usr/bin/flock -n /tmp/alert-processor.lock.
#   /opt/run-logs/alert-processor/ must exist — cron will not create it, and if the
#   redirect fails the shell exits before docker runs, leaving no .out file at all.
#
# ODD-JOBS (monthly)
#   Creates NEXT month's partitions for all 24 partitioned tables, so a failed run
#   leaves a full month to notice and fix before any insert is affected. That margin
#   only helps if the failure is VISIBLE — the 2026-08-01 run created nothing and went
#   unnoticed for three weeks behind /dev/null. This is why the single-> convention
#   exists.
#   No flock needed: the job self-gates. If next month is already complete AND no
#   archive cohort is due, it halts with a SEQUENCE HALTED event rather than re-issuing
#   DDL and reporting success it did not earn. Running it by hand off-schedule is
#   therefore harmless and obvious in the run log.
#   Every partitioned table on this host depends on this job, util.app_run_logs
#   included. A "no partition of relation ... found for row" error in ANY app means
#   this job failed or has not run.
#
# ODD-JOBS (test harness)
#   Uncomment for a cycle or two, then re-comment. By cadence it belongs in section 1
#   while active; it is parked in section 2 to stay beside the entry it exercises.
#   flock only because an every-minute cadence can pile up if a run hangs.
#   Writes to cron.pg-part-arch.TEST.out, NOT the monthly job's .out — with a single >
#   sharing the path would erase the last monthly run's evidence, which is exactly what
#   the 2026-08-01 incident says you want kept.
# =============================================================================
